gRPC Rich-Context Approach (Optimized Internal Hop)
---------------------------------------------------

Concept
- Same logical model as HTTP Rich-Context: controlplane returns AppAuthContext.
- Transport between dataplane and controlplane is gRPC with mTLS for lower latency and strong service authentication.

Flow
1. Dataplane AuthenticationHandler uses a gRPC client stub to call `GetAppAuthContext(AppIdRequest)`; includes mTLS client cert (preferred) or service JWT in metadata.
2. Controlplane gRPC service validates appId/secret, revocation, constraints; returns AppAuthContext protobuf.
3. Handler maps protobuf fields into RoutingContext keys, matching JWT branch semantics.

Pros
- Lower latency and smaller overhead vs HTTP+JSON.
- Built-in deadlines and retries via gRPC client.
- Strong mTLS identity; easier to enforce per-service authz.

Cons
- Requires gRPC infra (load balancer, cert distribution, observability).
- Additional deployment complexity compared to HTTP.

When to choose
- If controlplane already serves gRPC or infra can support it.
- High QPS machine-to-machine traffic where latency is sensitive.

Data contract (proto sketch)
message AppIdRequest { string app_id = 1; string app_secret = 2; }
message AppAuthContext {
  string app_id = 1;
  string role = 2;
  string iid = 3;
  string drl = 4;
  string did = 5;
  map<string, string> cons = 6;
  repeated string allowed_attributes = 7;
  string access_policy = 8;
  string owner_user_id = 9;
  int64 expires_at = 10;
  int64 revocation_version = 11;
}

Operational notes
- Enforce deadlines (e.g., 500 ms) and max retry count (1) in client.
- Export metrics: deadlineExceeded, unavailable, permissionDenied.
- Rotate mTLS certs via existing PKI; pin controlplane CA in dataplane.

